WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.150
Nine drift rules run against
the approved baseline.

2
00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:08.000
New and changed identities are warnings,
with the exact columns that changed.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.320
Ownerless identities are findings:

4
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.650
orphan candidates in Chapter 2's
run, and this code disables nothing.

5
00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:17.302
An app with no sign-in
for over ninety days,

6
00:00:17.302 --> 00:00:20.889
or a credential that expires
within thirty, is a warning;

7
00:00:20.889 --> 00:00:24.867
both numbers are Microsoft
Learn's, from its recommendations

8
00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:29.000
to remove unused applications
and renew expiring credentials.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.525
An enabled directory account whose
password never expires is a finding.

10
00:00:33.525 --> 00:00:37.050
So is a credential added outside
the pipeline, one per key:

11
00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.025
Microsoft Learn's security
operations guide for applications

12
00:00:41.025 --> 00:00:44.475
rates a credential added to
an existing app as high risk.

13
00:00:44.475 --> 00:00:47.766
A new consent grant or app
permission is a warning,

14
00:00:47.766 --> 00:00:51.137
or a finding when it's
tenant-wide or high privilege.

15
00:00:51.137 --> 00:00:53.321
And the ninth is the honest one:

16
00:00:53.321 --> 00:00:56.988
an evidence gap is reported,
never treated as a pass.

17
00:00:56.988 --> 00:00:59.617
The key rule, the
program's design guidance:

18
00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:04.878
a credential counts as inside the pipeline
only when two independent records agree,

19
00:01:04.878 --> 00:01:09.530
the listed pipeline identity and the
rotation pipeline's credential log.
